# Cleaning a copper coin, and the green that comes back
#
# A tarnished coin goes bright in vinegar. Does the salt matter to the green
# that appears afterwards? Two matched beakers change one thing, so the colour
# is evidence rather than a story we told after seeing it.
register lv2

# 1. Control: copper oxide and vinegar, but no chloride.
add v1 CuO 1g
add v1 white_vinegar_5_percent 30mL
look v1

# 2. Test: the same amounts in a fresh beaker, with salt as the only change.
new beaker
add v2 CuO 1g
add v2 white_vinegar_5_percent 30mL
add v2 NaCl 2g
look v2

# Compare the ledgers side by side. Chloride is what turns dissolved copper
# back into a green solid: atacamite, Cu2Cl(OH)3, the mineral that grows on
# copper fittings near the sea. The control keeps the chloride claim honest.
inspect v1
inspect v2

# What this shows and what it does not. The blue in the liquid is dissolved
# copper(II) and it is computed; the green solid is computed too, by the
# aqueous engine, from a phase the shipped database has always defined.
# What was missing until now was a species for it in this lab's registry —
# so the bench could see that the solution was supersaturated and could say
# so, and could not put anything at the bottom of the beaker.
#
# There is still no coin. This is the chemistry of a copper surface without
# the surface: no tarnish layer to remove, no shine to come back, and no
# distinction between a coin that has been dipped for a second and one left
# in overnight. What the bench has is what the liquid does.
